{"entries":{"chapter-37-study-sexuality-sexual-function-and-the-aging-man|Chapter 37 Study: Sexuality, Sexual Function, and the Aging Man":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Many men remain sexually active into late life; changes in health, partners and social circumstances affect activity as well as physiology (p547-549).":["u3"],"copy|Initiate a respectful discussion and assess the particular concern: erection, desire, performance anxiety or orgasm/ejaculation (p549, p551).":["u4"],"copy|Explore partner and psychological factors, depression, vascular disease, diabetes and medication effects (p550-551).":["u5"],"copy|Erectile dysfunction commonly has vascular or neurologic causes; low testosterone is not its universal explanation (p547, p550-551).":["u6"],"copy|In long-term care, support privacy and intimacy while assessing consent and capacity for the particular situation (p549).":["u7"],"copy|Address lifestyle factors, reversible disease and contributing medicines before direct treatment (p551).":["u8"],"copy|Sildenafil, vardenafil, tadalafil and avanafil have broadly similar efficacy; all require sexual stimulation (p551-552).":["u9"],"copy|Sildenafil/vardenafil: take on an empty stomach; food does not significantly impair tadalafil/avanafil absorption. Tadalafil lasts longer (p552).":["u10"],"copy|These agents are contraindicated with nitrates; caution with alpha blockers because of hypotension. Headache, flushing and dyspepsia are common (p552).":["u11"],"copy|Intracavernosal therapy or urethral alprostadil are alternatives; local pain, prolonged erections or fibrosis are relevant cautions (p552).":["u12"],"copy|Vacuum devices and prostheses offer nonoral options. Do not leave the constriction ring on beyond 30 minutes; use care with anticoagulation (p552).":["u13"],"copy|Prosthesis infection is an important complication; treatment choice should account for acceptability and practical use (p552).":["u14"],"copy|Diagnose hypogonadism from compatible symptoms/signs plus consistently low morning, preferably fasting, levels on at least two occasions (p552).":["u15"],"copy|Testosterone can modestly improve desire, activity and erectile function in hypogonadal men; it does not improve these outcomes in eugonadal men (p553).":["u16"],"copy|Adding testosterone after failure of an oral erectile-dysfunction drug may help severe hypogonadism, not normal testosterone status (p553).":["u17"],"copy|The chapter directs detailed testosterone evaluation and prescribing to the endocrine chapter; it supplies no universal dose here (p552-553).":["u18"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u19"],"copy|CHAPTER 37s / HAZZARD":["u20"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 37; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u21"],"copy|Assessment and context":["u22"],"copy|Treat erectile dysfunction":["u23"],"copy|Testosterone":["u24"],"copy|No past-exam questions are currently linked to this chapter.":["u25"],"copy|High-fat meals can delay the effect of sildenafil or vardenafil. Tadalafil and avanafil may be taken with or without food, although a high-fat meal can also delay peak avanafil concentration. Tadalafil has a longer window of action (p552). FDA avanafil prescribing information, §§2.1 and 12.3.":["u10"],"copy|The chapter directs that sildenafil and vardenafil be taken on an empty stomach because high-fat meals or alcohol delay absorption. Food does not significantly interfere with tadalafil or avanafil absorption, and tadalafil has a longer duration of action (p552). Current practice (not the book): Avanafil may be taken with or without food. A high-fat meal can delay peak concentration. FDA prescribing information, 2022, §§2.1, 12.3.":["u10"],"copy|In the practice case the man already has exertional symptoms (p550, Figure 37-2).":["u60"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Evaluate possible ischemia and exercise tolerance before treatment.":["u61"],"copy|Stress testing can clarify indeterminate risk when clinically appropriate.":["u62"],"copy|The chapter states that neither hemodialysis nor testosterone replacement has been shown to improve erectile dysfunction in men with renal failure.":["u166"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): A small uncontrolled series found improved erection scores during testosterone treatment of hypogonadal men receiving dialysis.":["u167"],"copy|Causation is uncertain.":["u168"],"copy|The book advises caution with testosterone in untreated obstructive sleep apnea (Ch 97 p1534).":["u129"],"copy|1534":["u169"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Defer testosterone in untreated severe obstructive sleep apnea.":["u170"],"copy|Endocrine Society, recommendation 2.2.":["u132"],"copy|CHAPTER 37 / HAZZARD":["u20"]},"maxId":170,"sequenceOffsets":[[171,-145],[205,-141],[224,-137],[227,-141],[228,-138],[267,-137],[269,-136],[302,0]]}
